ange(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "50000"} "1912"] "\n" [:option {:value "50009"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "50018"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "50027"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "50036"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "50045"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "50054"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "50063"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "50081"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "50099"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "50117"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "50135"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "50153"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "50171"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "50189"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "50207"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "50225"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "50243"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "50261"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "50270"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "50288"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "260915"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "317000"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "351850"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "2003825"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/361"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Individual, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "5 –  7 August 2021"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2124"} "Musashino Forest Sports Plaza, Chōfu, Tokyo, Japan"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/2112"} "Tokyo Stadium, Tokyo, Japan"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19002500"} "36 from 26 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Scoring by point tables."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The men’s modern pentathlon event in Tokyo was again contested over two days with 36 athletes competing. Of the medallists from Rio only " [:a {:href "/athletes/116463"} "Pavlo Tymoshchenko"] " participated again. As the World Championships are an annual event, there had been four editions since Rio (the 2020 event was cancelled due to the COVID-19 pandemic), with the champions being " [:a {:href "/athletes/123519"} "Jeong Jin-Hwa"] " (2017), " [:a {:href "/athletes/134029"} "Jamie Cooke"] " (2018), " [:a {:href "/athletes/133876"} "Valentin Belaud"] " (2019), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/126566"} "Ádám Marosi"] " (2021). The 2021 Worlds were held in June, less than two months before the Olympics, with the other medallists being " [:a {:href "/athletes/2104239"} "Aleksandr Lifanov"] " (silver) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/2502916"} "Ahmed El-Gendy"] " (bronze). Lifanov (2014) and El-Gendy (2018) had also been the gold medallists at the last two Youth Olympic Games. The leader of the world rankings just before the Games was Marosi, followed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/133877"} "Valentin Prades"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/134973"} "Jeon Ung-Tae"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The traditional fencing round-robin was contested on the first day. " [:a {:href "/athletes/134030"} "Joe Choong"] " and Lifanov took the lead with 25 wins each, and behind them were " [:a {:href "/athletes/141092"} "Illia Palazkov"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/121732"} "Justinas Kinderis"] ", each with 24 wins. After a break of one day the competition, was continued with the 200 m swimming, which was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/116447"} "Amro El-Geziry"] " in a time of 1:52.96. The Britons Cook, Olympic record holder in swimming in a 50 m pool in modern pentathlon, and Choong finished second and third. After the following bonus round in fencing, Choong was in the lead with 573 points, with Jeong Jin-Hwa second (554) and Palazkov third (552). The riding element was eagerly awaited, particularly after the dramatic events in the women’s competition the previous day. The best riders were " [:a {:href "/athletes/134217"} "Patrick Dogue"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/2102966"} "Gustav Gustenau"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/2103255"} "Martin Vlach"] " who all collected the maximum of 300 points. The leader Choong showed a solid performance after being drawn with " [:em {} "Clntino"] ", the same horse that help compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/130257"} "Kate French"] " win gold the day before. Choong was still in the lead after three events with 859 points, and Jeong Jin-Hwa remained second (847 points) and was followed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/133426"} "Jan Kuf"] " (838)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The scored points were converted into times and gave the following gaps for the laser-run: Choong started 12 seconds in front of Jeong Jin-Hwa, while Kuf was another nine seconds behind. Jeon Ung-Tae (+0.28) led the rest of the field with the other athletes close behind with a distance of a few seconds from each other. After the second shooting, Choong was still in the lead followed by Kuf and the two Koreans. Already in fifth position was El-Geziry who started 13th with +0:50 behind Choong. At the 2000 m split Choong was about six seconds ahead of a group consisting of Kuf, El-Gendy, Jeon Ung-Tae, and Jeong Jin-Hwa. Up to the last shooting Choong had controlled the race when El-Gendy caught up the Briton and overtook him. Choong, however, was able to fight back and with a 300 m spurt to the finish line secured the gold medal five seconds in front of El-Gendy. The battle for bronze was a fight between the two Koreans, who had stayed close together for a long time during the race, with the better finish for Jeon Ung-Tae, who was able to make up the 16 seconds his compatriot Jeong Jin-Hwa led him by at one stage, to finally win bronze, four seconds ahead. The best performance in the laser-run was by Vlach with a total time of 10:30.13 to finish fifth overall."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Choong won the first ever individual modern pentathlon medal for Great Britain. El-Gendy, who was the youngest ever competitor in this event, also won the sport’s first ever medal for Egypt, as well as for the continent of Africa, and Jeon Ung-Tae won South Korea’s first ever medal in this sport."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Swimming"] [:th {} "Fencing"] [:th {} "Riding"] [:th {} "Running &amp; Shooting"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/134030"} "Joe Choong"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "1482"] [:td {} "321 (3)"] [:td {} "25 (1)"] [:td {} "286 (14)"] [:td {} "623 (15)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2502916"} "Ahmed El-Gendy"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EGY"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EGY.png"}] "EGY"]] [:td {} "1477"] [:td {} "316 (5)"] [:td {} "18 (15)"] [:td {} "284 (18)"] [:td {} "668 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/134973"} "Jeon Ung-Tae"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"]] [:td {} "1470"] [:td {} "316 (6)"] [:td {} "21 (9)"] [:td {} "289 (11)"] [:td {} "639 (7)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/123519"} "Jeo